<!--
    angr "who condensing opt reversion" (test_who_condensing_opt_reversion,
    x86_64/decompiler/who.o::scan_entries) -> the SAILR CrossJumpReverter.

    Compilers perform Irreducible Statement Condensing (ISC, "cross jumping"):
    identical tail code reached from several predecessors is condensed into ONE
    shared basic block, and all but one predecessor reach it by a goto.  In
    who.o::scan_entries the two identical `v1 = *a1;` reload tails (the
    `need_deadprocs && v1 == 8` case and the skip/else path) were condensed into
    one shared block, so kuna emits exactly one residual goto:

        else if (need_deadprocs and v1 == 8) {
            print_deadprocs(a1);
            goto label_4012b5;            // (the residual cross-jump goto)
        }
        ...
        else { label_4012b5: v1 = *a1; }  // (shared, condensed tail)
        if (v1 == 2) { ... }              // (both paths converge here)

    angr's CrossJumpReverter (angr/analyses/decompiler/optimization_passes/
    cross_jump_reverter.py; Basque et al., USENIX Security 2024 "SAILR") reverts
    the condensing by DUPLICATING the shared tail back into the goto source so both
    paths fall straight through and the goto/label vanish.  This is the sibling of
    the `gotoreduce` option (which duplicates *return* tails, a closed path); the
    cross-jump variant duplicates a NON-return, fall-through tail and additionally
    proves a convergence precondition (the goto's structured fall-through already
    reaches the tail's successor) so the duplication is semantics-preserving.

    Minimised x86-64 reproducer (gcc layout, faithful to scan_entries' CFG): a
    do/while loop over a gated cascade of `if (v1 == K) { call print_K; v1 = *p; }`
    arms (each with its own reload, jumping forward to the convergence test), plus
    the deadprocs arm gated by a COMPOUND guard `if ((need && v1 == 8))` whose body
    is `call print_8;` then a BACKWARD goto into the shared `v1 = *p` reload tail
    (which the skip/else path falls into).  That backward cross-jump into a shared,
    fall-through tail is exactly the ISC-condensed shape Ghidra's CollapseStructure
    (and kuna's region structurer) must express as a goto.

      cj:    loop { eax = gate();
                    if (!eax) { shared: v1=*p; goto conv; }
                    v1 = *p;
                    if (v1==1){call sub1; v1=*p; goto conv}
                    else if (v1==3){call sub3; v1=*p; goto conv}
                    else if (v1==5){call sub5; v1=*p; goto conv}
                    else if (v1==6){call sub6; v1=*p; goto conv}
                    else if (need && v1==8){call sub8; goto shared}  // <- cross jump
              conv: if (v1==2) sink(); p+=0x40; } while(p);
      gate/sub1/sub3/sub5/sub6/sub8/sink: ret stubs.

    Pass 1 (option crossjumprevert off, the DEFAULT): the deadprocs arm renders
    `else if ((a1 != 0) && (v1 == 8)) { sub8(); goto label_X; }` with a labelled
    shared tail `label_X: v1 = *a0;` -> assertions P1 (the goto) and P1b (the
    label).
    Pass 2 (option crossjumprevert on): the shared tail is duplicated back into the
    deadprocs arm (`sub8(); v1 = *a0;`), the goto/label vanish, and a
    `crossjumprevert:` warning is logged -> assertion P2 (the log).  P3 pins the
    TOTAL goto-label count across both passes at exactly one (one in pass 1, zero in
    pass 2): the reversion removed the only goto.
-->
